## INTRODUCTION
Many of the products on which we rely every day, such as plastics, fragrances, and pharmaceuticals, are manufactured using catalysts.1 By definition, a catalyst accelerates the rate of a chemical reaction but is not consumed during the course of the reaction.2 However, all catalysts have finite lifetimes and limited turnover numbers (TONs), as they can decompose via a number of pathways. The most common catalyst decomposition pathways involve multimetallic processes and ligand-centered chemistry (Figure 1a).3 In homogeneous catalysis, bulky substituents are commonly installed around the metal centers to prevent metal−metal contact via steric hindrance, thus avoiding multimetallic decomposition pathways. However, these bulky substituents can undergo ligandcentered chemistry, such as $\mathrm{C-H}$ activation,4 arene coordination,5 and dehydrogenation, leading to catalyst decomposition.6 Moreover, steric hindrance can impede substrate coordination to metal centers and attenuate catalytic activities. An alternative strategy is to site-isolate catalytic species in a well-defined environment to avoid multimetallic pathways and ligand-centered chemistry.
Nature is the earliest adopter of catalytic site isolation in enzymes.7 Protein folding creates an environment for high catalytic activity and specificity by keeping a substrate in close proximity to the active metal species via molecular recognition (Figure 1b). Enzymes are used in some industrial processes, such as the isomerization of D-glucose to D-fructose by xylose isomerase and the hydrolytic transformation of acrylonitrile to acrylamide by nitrile hydratase. However, the catalytic activity and specificity of enzymes are highly sensitive to the surrounding media, with their activity being highly suppressed in nonaqueous solutions, which severely limits the broad application of enzymes in industrial organic synthesis.
Synthetic systems have been created to site-isolate homogeneous catalysts. For example, dendrimers have been used to incorporate catalytic functionalities on the exterior of the branches or in the core of the dendrimers.8 Knapen and von Koten demonstrated the Kharasch addition of polyhaloalkanes across a $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{C}}$ bond by Ni complexes immobilized on the branches of a dendrimer.9 Dendrimers with catalytic cores containing Pd, Rh, Cu, Ni, Ru, and others are also know n.10 The microenvironments surrounding the catalytic sites in high-generation dendrimers are nonuniform because of the introduction of defects in the repetitive steps of dendrimer synthesis.8 Access to the active sites is also hindered in large dendrimers with globular structures. Both of these issues limit practical applications of dendrimer-based catalysts.
Metal oxide surfaces have been used to support catalysts with a high site density.11 Site isolation can be achieved by templating the surface with barriers and by grafting molecular species with bulky ligands on dehydroxylated surfaces. The separation of catalysts on metal oxide surfaces prevents agglomeration and decomposition, but the nonuniform distribution of catalytic sites makes it difficult to characterize the catalytic species and to study the reaction mechanism. This Account highlights the design of metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) with site-isolated active catalysts and their catalytic performance in organic reactions.
Over the past two decades, MOFs have attracted significant attention from a broad spectrum of scientists and engineers12 and inspired the exploration of their applications in diverse areas such as gas storage,13,14 gas separation, 15 nanomedicine,16 and catalysis.17,18 MOFs have in particular provided a unique platform to design recyclable and reusable single-site solid catalysts that inherit the molecular tunability of homogeneous catalysts via a limitless combination of metal centers and organic linkers. Compared with classical heterogeneous catalysts, MOF catalysts feature a uniform distribution of identical catalytic species, which facilitates the identification of active catalysts and the study of reaction mechanisms. Although all three components of MOFs, namely, inorganic nodes,19,20 organic linkers,21,22 and channels,23,24 have been utilized as active sites or to tether catalytic centers for a wide variety of organic reactions, in this Account we focus on the functionalization of organic ligands in MOFs with siteisolated active catalysts.
Many types of MOFs have been synthesized since the emergence of this field.25,26 In the early years, MOFs were mostly based on divalent metals, such as $\dot{\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}},\mathsf{Z n}^{\mathrm{II}},$ and $\mathrm{Cd}^{\mathrm{II}},$ which exhibit limited hydrolytic and thermal stabilities, $27-29$ except in a few cases where the MOF porosity is very limited.30 The advent of MOFs based on high-valent metals, such as $\mathsf{A l}^{\mathrm{III}}$ , $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{{IV}}},$ and $\mathrm{Zr}^{\mathrm{IV}31-33}$ has significantly increased MOF stability. Constructed from $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ nodes and dicarboxylate linkers, the most studied $\mathrm{z}\mathrm{r}$ MOFs with the UiO structure remain stable until ${\sim}350\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and tolerate a broad range of solvents and reagents.34 The enhanced stability of high-valent MOFs makes them ideal supports for site-isolated catalysts (Figure 1c). Over the past decade, many different functionalized linkers have been developed by Lin and co-workers to construct a library of reusable MOF catalysts for asymmetric catalysis and photocatalysis that feature superior catalytic performance over their homogeneous analogues. In this Account, we present our recent research progress in developing ligand-supported single-site MOF catalysts for challenging organic reactions. Monophosphine-, bipyridine-, phenanthroline-, $\beta$ -diketimine-, and salicylaldimine-based ligands have been used to support both precious (Pd, Pt, Ir, Ru) and earthabundant (Cu, Co, Fe) metals to generate single-site solid catalysts that significantly outperform their homogeneous counterparts or, in some cases, do not have homogeneous counterparts (Figure 2).
## MONOPHOSPHINE-BASED MOFS SUPPORT SINGLE-SITE PRECIOUS METAL CATALYSTS
Sterically hindered and electron-rich phosphines have played a pivotal role in supporting organometallic catalysts for many important reactions. For example, Wilkinson’s catalyst RhCl$\left(\mathrm{PPh}_{3}\right)_{3}$ for alkene hydrogenation and hydroformylation, Crabtree’s catalyst $[\mathrm{Ir}(\mathrm{py})(\mathsf{\bar{c}o d})(\mathrm{PCy}_{3})]\mathrm{pF}_{6}$ for alkene and ketone hydrogenation, and Grubb’s first-generation catalyst $\mathrm{Ru(=\mathrm{CHPh})\bar{C l}_{2}(\mathrm{PC}y_{3})_{2}}$ for olefin metathesis all use phosphines to stabilize catalytic metal centers.35 Phosphines are ideal for supporting precious metal catalysts because of their strong binding affinity, which prevents catalyst decomposition into metal nanoparticles or metal oxides. Such strongbinding phosphines can also lead to detrimental overcoordination with two or more phosphines on each metal center, which inhibits substrate binding and significantly diminishes the catalytic activity. For example, the Suzuki− Miyaura coupling of chloropyridine and $o$ -tolylboronic acid is highly dependent on the Pd/phosphine ratio. The thermodynamically stable $\big[\mathrm{Pd}\big(\mathrm{P}^{t}\mathrm{B}\mathbf{u}_{3}\big)_{2}\big]$ species displays poor catalytic activity for the coupling reaction. Addition of nonligated palladium precursor to $\left[\bar{\mathrm{Pd}}(\mathrm{P}^{t}\mathrm{B}\mathbf{u}_{3})_{2}\right]$ produces the much more catalytically active $\left[\mathrm{Pd}\left(\mathrm{P}^{t}\mathrm{B}\mathbf{u}_{3}\right)\right]$ species.36 The stabilization of monophosphine-ligated precious metal catalysts through ligand design is thus crucial for optimal catalytic performance. This concept was elegantly demonstrated in Buchwald’s dialkylbiarylphosphine ligands, which disfavor the formation of the $\mathrm{PdL}_{2}$ species37 and have found broad applications in Buchwald− Hartwig and Suzuki−Miyaura cross-coupling reactions.
Sawano et al.38 designed a triarylphosphine-based tricarboxylic acid and synthesized a porous triarylphosphine-based MOF $\left(\mathrm{P_{1}}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{MOF}\right)$ under solvothermal conditions. Metalation of the $\mathbf{P}_{1}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{F}$ with different metal precursors afforded monophosphine-ligated metal complexes that are highly active in catalyzing a broad range of reactions without the installation of bulky substituents.38
Wilkinson’s catalyst is well-known for hydrogenation of simple olefins but inactive for tetrasubstituted olefins because of the steric hindrance of the three coordinating triphenylphosphine groups (Figure 3c). The $\mathbf{P}_{1}{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{F}$ metalated with $\bar{[}\mathrm{Rh}\bar{\mathrm{Cl}}(\mathrm{nbd}\bar{)}]_{2},$ namely, $\mathbf{P_{\mathrm{1^{-MOF-Rh}}}}$ , catalyzed the hydrogenation of tetramethylethylene with a TON of $81000$ at room temperature. For other alkene substrates, including 1- octene, styrene, cyclohexene, and $\alpha$ -methylstyrene, $\mathrm{P_{1}{\mathrm{-}}M O F}.$ - Rh displayed even greater activities. The Rh coordination environment within the MOF was unambiguously identified to be $\mathrm{Rh(Cl)(nbd)(P_{1}\mathrm{{-}M O F)}}$ by single-crystal X-ray diffraction (Figure 3a). ${\bf P}_{1}$ -MOF-Rh was reused 20 times in the hydrogenation of 1-octene without significant loss in catalytic performance, indicating the robustness and recyclability of the MOF catalyst. $\mathrm{{P}}_{1}$ -MOF-Rh was also highly active in the hydrosilylation of alkenes and carbonyls, with maximum TONs of 820 000 and 186 000, respectively. For both reactions, the catalyst was reused for nine runs without significant loss of activity (Figure 3d).
$\mathrm{P_{1}{\mathrm{-}}M O F}$ was also metalated with $\left[\mathrm{Ir}(\mathrm{OMe})(\mathrm{cod})\right]_{2}$ to generate $\mathrm{P_{1}{-}M O F{-}I r}$ for arene $\mathrm{C-H}$ borylation reactions (Figure 3b). This reaction was first tested with Ir−phosphine compounds in the pioneering works of $\sinh^{39}$ and Miyaura and Hartwig40 and has provided a powerful method to generate arylboronates, the substrates of Suzuki−Miyaura reactions to access biphenyl compounds. One limitation of the reaction is the high cost of Ir. The development of a reusable solid catalyst with activity and selectivity similar to those of the homogeneous counterpart could make the reaction more economical. With 0.1 mol $\%$ $\mathbf{P}_{\mathrm{1}}{\mathrm{-MOF-Ir}},$ borylation of benzene at $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $40\mathrm{~h~}$ gave PhBpin in $84\%$ yield. The homogeneous Ir−phosphine control, $\mathrm{Ir}\mathrm{(OMe)}$ - (cod) $\left(\mathrm{PPh}_{3}\right)$ , afforded the product in only $13\%$ yield under identical conditions, possibly because of bimolecular catalyst decomposition. $\mathrm{P_{1}{-}M O F{-}I r}$ is also active for the borylation of toluene, methyl benzoate, and 1,2-dichlorobenzene.
Several other MOFs have also been prepared by incorporating phosphine functionalities such as triphenylphosphine and diphenylphoshine derivatives.41−43 Grafting of $\mathbf{Au}^{\mathrm{I}}$ onto a Ca−triphenylphosphine MOF at the available phosphorus site enabled 1-hexene/ $\overset{\cdot}{n}$ -hexane separation.44 In porous coordination materials (PCMs) consisting of $Z\mathrm{r}$ and triphenylphosphine, immobilized ${\mathrm{Au}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ catalysts were active for cyclization and hydration reactions.45 Ru and Cu ions anchored to diphenylphosphine ligands catalyzed the dehydrogenation of formic acid46 and the ketalization of ethylene glycol,42 respectively. However, these systems experienced substantial leaching of the metal centers.41
## BIPYRIDINE-BASED MOFS SUPPORT SINGLE-SITE PRECIOUS METAL CATALYSTS
Common N-donor ligands include bipyridine (Bpy),47,48 phenanthroline (PT),49 β-diketimine (NacNac),50,51 dipyrrin,52 and pincer ligands such as pyridinediimine (PDI).53 Ndonor ligands are not as popular as phosphine ligands in precious metal catalysis since the relatively weaker coordination strength makes the catalyst more prone to decomposition. Very bulky protecting groups are typically installed on the Ndonor ligands to prevent oligomerization and metal−ligand redistributions, as displayed in the ligands summarized in Figure 4a. Such bulky protecting groups can inhibit substrate access to the metal centers and slow the reaction kinetics.54 Immobilization of N-donor ligands onto MOF materials provides a novel approach to generate site-isolated Ncoordinated catalysts without relying on bulky protecting groups.
Manna et al. used Bpy-based MOFs to immobilize Ir and Pd catalysts for several important organic transfromations.55,56
Bpy-UiO has the formula $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{(OH)}_{4}\mathrm{\big[Bpy(CO_{2})_{2}\big]}_{6}$ and is isostructural to UiO-67, a very stable MOF with a combination of tetragonal pores and larger octahedral pores. Metalation of Bpy-UiO with $\big[\mathrm{Ir}\big(\mathrm{OMe}\big)\big(\mathrm{\bar{C}O D}\big)\big]_{2}$ gives Bpy-UiO-Ir, with a $30\%$ loading of Ir with respect to Bpy (Figure 4b). The MOF particle morphology and crystallinity remain unchanged after the metalation, as indicated by transmission electron microscopy and powder X-ray diffraction. Bpy-UiO-Ir is very active for arene $\mathrm{C-H}$ borylation. With $0.5\mathrm{\mol\}\%$ Ir, Bpy-UiOIr selectively borylates $m$ -xylene in $94\%$ yield with exclusive meta selectivity (Figure 4c). The catalyst also shows a broad substrate scope, allowing the borylation of a wide range of unactivated arenes, including those containing alkoxy groups and halide substituents (Figure 5a). Remarkably, Bpy-UiO-Ir was recovered and reused at least 20 times for the borylation of $m$ -xylene without a significant decrease in catalyst performance. Thus, Bpy-UiO-Ir is a highly active and economical catalyst for arene $\mathrm{C-H}$ borylation and has the potential to further broaden the application of this reaction in industrial processes.
Bpy-UiO-Ir, mBPV-UiO-Ir, and mPT-UiO-Ir were also shown to be highly active catalysts for tandem carbonyl hydrosilylation and directed $\mathrm{C-H}$ silylation to give benzoxasilole products in almost quantitative yields (Figure 5b). mBPV-UiO and mPT-UiO are elongated versions of the BpyUiO MOF with two extra ethynyl and phenyl groups inserted into the Bpy and PT linkers, respectively (Figure 4b). This reaction was first reported by Simmons and Hartwig in 2010.57 Benzoxasiloles are useful synthetic precursors for generating phenols through Tamao−Fleming oxidation and biaryl compounds through Hiyama cross-coupling. mPT-UiO-Ir was recycled and reused at least 15 times for benzoxasilole generation without significant loss of catalytic activity. Moreover, the homogeneous Ir catalyst generally requires a higher catalyst loading $(1\mathrm{\mol\}\%$ ) and a hydrogen acceptor (norbonene). mPT-UiO-Ir catalyzes the reaction at much lower catalyst loadings $(0.025{-}0.1\ \mathrm{mol}\ \%)$ without the need for a hydrogen acceptor. A broad range of substrates were transformed by the catalyst, including those containing halides, alkoxides, and even thiophene groups.
Bpy-UiO was treated with $\mathrm{Pd}\bar{(\mathrm{MeCN})}_{4}(\mathrm{BF}_{4})_{2}$ to afford BpyUiO-Pd, with a Pd loading of $24\%$ with respect to the Bpy functionality (Figure 6). The Pd coordination environment in
Bpy-UiO-Pd was proposed to be $\mathrm{\big[Pd\big(MeCN\big)_{2}\big(B p y–U i O\big)\big]}.$ - $\left(\bar{\mathrm{BF_{4}}}\right)_{2}$ . Inspired by the work of Stahl on the [ $(6,6^{\prime}.$ - $\mathrm{dmbpy)Pd(\bar{M}e C N)_{2}\bar{]}(T F A)_{2}}$ -catalyzed aerobic oxidation of cyclohexanone to phenol,58 Bpy-UiO-Pd was tested for the same reaction to examine the effect of Bpy immobilization on the catalyst performance. For the oxidation of cyclohexanone, the best catalyst performance was obtained at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with 1.0 mol $\%$ catalyst to give phenol in $93\%$ yield.56 Methyl-, ethyl-, and phenyl-substituted cyclohexanones were oxidized to the corresponding phenols in $83-91\%$ yield. Compared with the homogeneous control $[(\mathrm{CO_{2}M e)_{2}B p y})\mathrm{Pd(MieCN)_{4}][B F_{4}]_{2}},$ Bpy-UiO-Pd showed 3 times higher activity, indicating the beneficial effect of catalyst immobilization.
N-donor ligands, including $\mathrm{Bpy}^{59-61}$ and PT,62 have been used to prepare other MOF materials. The Al-based Bpy MOF, MOF-253, was reported in $2010^{59}$ and metalated with Ru for catalytic alcohol oxidations in 2012.63 Functionalization of the Bpy ligand in UiO systems with Pd afforded a MOF catalyst for Suzuki−Miyaura coupling.60 Immobilization of Cu produced a selective oxidation catalyst for the transformation of cyclooctene to the corresponding epoxide.61 A Zr−PT MOF functionalized with Ir was recently shown to be active for methane borylation.62 The Bpy- and PT-based MOFs have thus provided powerful supports for transition metal catalysis. The M−Bpy moiety inside the MOF channel displays higher catalyst activity and stability than its homogeneous counterpart.
## BIPYRIDINE-BASED MOFS SUPPORT SINGLE-SITEBASE METAL CATALYSTS
Motivated by the success of Bpy-UiO-Pd in catalytic cyclohexenone oxidation,56 Co ions were coordinated to Bpy-UiO to generate an earth-abundant metal catalyst. The direct replacement of second- and third-row precious metals with first-row base metals can be challenging, as precious metals often undergo two-electron processes, such as oxidative addition and reductive elimination, while base metals commonly involve one-electron processes.64 One strategy to induce two-electron processes in base-metal catalysis relies on the use of redox-noninnocent $\pi$ -acceptor ligandssuch as Bpy,
PT, terpyridine $(\mathrm{Tpy})$ , and PDIwhich can stabilize low-spin, low-oxidation-state species and/or participate in redox chemistry via radical formation.65 Some base-metal-centered olefin hydrogenation catalysts have recently been reported,64−66 with most of them featuring Co complexes supported by diphosphine ligands or pincer-type ligands. Because these N-donor ligands coordinate strongly to base metals, they are highly susceptible to overcoordination. In many instances, the catalysts disproportionate to form a bischelate metal species and metal nanoparticles (Figure 7a).
All of the aforementioned ligands require the installation of highly substituted protecting groups to prevent decomposition of homogeneous catalysts. Lin and co-workers have discovered that coordination of base metals to Bpy-type ligands in a MOF prevents the disproportionation reaction while facilitating catalysis.
Zhang et al.67 and Thacker et al.68 have used Bpy-UiO and mPT-UiO to support Co and Fe catalysts. These metalated MOFs (Bpy-UiO-Co and mPT-UiO-Fe, respectively) catalyzed a range of addition and $\mathrm{C-H}$ activation reactions (Figure 7b). Compared with homogeneous catalysts, which achieved low yields $\left(<15\%\right)$ for the hydrogenation of 2,3-dimethylbut-2- ene ,65,66 Bpy-UiO-Co achieved 76% yield at temperatures between 50 and $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a catalyst loading of $1.0\mathrm{mol}\%$ . At 0.1 1 $\mathrm{\nol\\%\Co,}$ , terminal alkenes were hydrogenated in $100\%$ yield in $<24\mathrm{~h~}$ at room temperature. Impressively, a TON of $2.5\times$ $10^{6}$ was achieved for 1-octene hydrogenation. A homogeneous control catalyst required higher loadings (up to $2{\mathrm{~mol~}}\%{\mathrm{~Co}}^{\cdot}$ ) and longer reaction times to achieve similar results.64 BpyUiO-Co was reused 16 times for 1-octene hydrogenation without a loss of catalytic activity and tolerated a wide range of functional groups.
Activation of the $_{\mathrm{Bpy-UiO-CoCl}_{2}}$ precatalyst with $\mathrm{NaBEt}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ afforded Bpy-UiO- ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{THF}})_{2}$ via reduction with elimination of $\mathrm{H}_{2},$ as confirmed by hydrogen quantification and extended Xray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) analysis. In contrast, the homogeneous analogue $\mathrm{Co}\big(\mathrm{^{Me_{2}}B p y)C l_{2}}$ disproportionated into Co nanoparticles and $\mathrm{Co}\big(\mathrm{^{Me_{2}}B p y)_{2}}$ upon activation with $\mathrm{NaBEt}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ . $\mathrm{x}$ -ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) and density functional theory (DFT) studies suggested $(\mathrm{Bpy}^{\bullet-})\mathrm{Co}^{\dot{\mathrm{I}}}(\mathrm{THF})_{2}$ as the resting state of the catalyst, showing that the Bpy ligand stabilizes $C_{0}$ in a low oxidation state.
In addition to hydrogenation reactions, mPT-UiO-Co catalyzed hydroboration reactions of challenging terminal and internal alkenes. Prop-1-en-2-ylbenzene was borylated in high yield $(82\%)$ , in contrast to a $\mathrm{P-N-N}$ pincer catalyst that showed no reaction with the alkene.64 The MOF was selective for the anti-Markovnikov alkylboronate product when terminal alkenes were used as substrates. Catalytic hydroboration of ketones and aldehydes is a facile method for the reduction of carbonyl groups to alcohols. mPT-UiO-Co catalyzed this reaction with very high catalytic activities at room temperature. High reactivity was also observed for $\mathrm{C-H}$ amination reactions catalyzed by mPT-MOF-Fe.68
## $\beta$ -DIKETIMINE-BASED MOFS SUPPORT SINGLE-SITE BASE METAL CATALYSTS
$\beta$ -Diketimine ligands, also known as NacNac ligands, are commonly used to generate metal complexes because of their strongly coordinating nitrogen moieties.69,70 These ligands stabilize a variety of metal centers with a range of oxidation states. Functionalization of the ligand backbone can influence the steric and electronic properties of the resulting metal complexes. However, when the functional group is rather small, polymeric clusters can form via phenoxide, amido, or halide bridges (Figure 8c).71 Site isolation provided by MOFs prevents this cluster formation.
Thacker et al.72 synthesized a MOF composed of $\beta\mathrm{.}$ - diketimine ligands, NacNac-UiO, to stabilize low-coordinate metal complexes. The NacNac functionality served as a handle for creating an active site that is orthogonal to the bridging ligand. Upon coordination with Fe, Cu, and Co, metalated NacNac-UiO was active for intramolecular $\mathrm{C}(\mathrm{sp}^{3})\mathrm{-H}$ amination, intermolecular $\mathrm{C}(\mathrm{sp}^{3})\mathrm{-H}$ amination, and alkene hydrogenation, respectively. The resulting catalysts featured low steric bulk and low coordination.
The Fe center in NacNac-UiO-Fe adopts a trigonal coordination environment with one NacNac ligand and one methyl group. The MOF catalyzed intramolecular $\mathrm{C}(\mathrm{sp}^{3})\mathrm{-H}$ amination at 3 times the activity of its homogeneous analogue. NacNac-UiO-Fe converted azides containing bulky phenyl groups and reactive functional groups to N-heterocycles that were unattainable using the homogeneous analogue.72 Literature reports of a homogeneous iron−dipyrinnato catalyst showed modest yields $(<60\%)$ for the amination of tert-butyl 2-methylpyrrolidine-1-carboxylate, tert-butyl 2-vinylpyrrolidine-1-carboxylate, and tert-butyl 2-phenylpyrrolidine-1-carboxylate,52 while NacNac-UiO-Fe achieved over $90\%$ yield for each of the products at half of the catalyst loading. 52
NacNac-UiO-Co features a square planar coordination environment with one NacNac, one hydride, and one THF ligand. The MOF catalyzed alkene hydrogenation with TONs as high as 700 000. The catalyst tolerated terminal and internal alkenes and was recycled and reused 10 times without a loss of activity. In contrast, the homogeneous analogue was inactive for the reaction.72
NacNac-UiO-Cu featured trigonal coordination with one NacNac ligand and one THF ligand and catalyzed sizeselective intermolecular $\mathrm{C}(\mathrm{sp}^{3})\mathrm{-H}$ amination of aniline. Timedependent catalytic conversion studies indicated that the MOF catalyst steadily produced the desired product while the homogeneous analogue deactivated quickly, likely via the combination of two $\mathrm{Cu}$ −anilides to form diazene (Figure 8c).67 NacNac-UiO-Cu was reused five times without a loss of activity.
## SALICYLALDIMINE-BASED MOFS SUPPORTSINGLE-SITE BASE METAL CATALYSTS
Salicylaldimine (Sal) ligands have commonly been used to stabilize Ni or $\mathrm{Zr}$ centers to catalyze olefin polymerization reactions.73 However, most of these catalysts require the installation of bulky substituents around the metal center to prevent ligand migrations to form catalytically inactive $\mathbf{M}(\mathrm{Sal})_{2}$ or $\mathbf{M}(\mathrm{Sa}\breve{1})_{3}$ species. Unfortunately, the presence of bulky groups compromises the catalytic performance. This behavior likely explains why Sal ligands are rarely used in other catalytic reactions.74
Manna et al.75 synthesized Sal-UiO-M $\left(\mathbf{M}\ =\ \mathrm{Fe},\ \mathrm{Co},\ \right)$ to stabilize the $\mathbf{M}{-}S{\mathrm{al}}$ complexes (Figure $9\mathrm{a}$ ). Activation of Sal
UiO-Fe with $\mathrm{NaBEt}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ afforded a highly active hydrogenation catalyst. Terminal alkenes, geminal-disubstituted alkenes, and dialkenes were all hydrogenated by the MOF catalyst without any alkene isomerization side reactions. The catalyst tolerated aldehyde, ketone, and ester functional groups. Interestingly, complete conversion of 1-octene, styrene, and prop-1-en-2- ylbenzene was achieved in $<18\mathrm{~h~}$ using less than $0.01\mathrm{\mol\}\%$ Fe, whereas a $2\mathrm{mol}\ \%$ loading of a homogeneous Co catalyst with a $\mathrm{{\DeltaP-N{-}P}}$ pincer ligand was required to achieve similar conversions.64 A catalytically inactive $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}(\mathrm{Sal})_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{4}(\mathrm{THF})_{2}$ cluster was obtained via oligomerization through $\mathrm{Cl}$ and O bridging ligands when attempts were made to prepare a homogeneous analogue of Sal-UiO-Fe (Figure 9b).75 The MOF catalyst was reused 20 times for the hydrogenation of 1- octene. Sal-MOF-Co catalyzed the hydrogenation of alkenes with a similar substrate scope, albeit with slightly lower yields and TONs (Figure 9c).
## CONCLUSION AND FUTURE OUTLOOK
We summarize in this Account the recent progress in designing ligand-supported single-site MOF catalysts for challenging organic reactions. MOF catalysts built from monophosphine-, bipyridine-, $\beta$ -diketimine-, and salicylaldimine-based ligands that are complexed to both precious $(\mathrm{Pd},\mathrm{Pt},\mathrm{Ir},\mathrm{Ru})$ ) and earthabundant $(\mathrm{Cu},$ Co, Fe) metals have been examined for a number of interesting catalytic reactions. It has become clear that MOF catalysts have the potential to transform commodity and fine chemical synthesis. The site isolation in MOFs allows the catalysts to remain active over a long period, affording high TONs. Impressive results have been achieved in $\mathrm{C-H}$ activation reactions, with catalytic activities rivaling or exceeding those of important homogeneous catalysts. MOF catalysts can be easily separated and reused over multiple catalytic cycles without a loss of activity. As the field of MOF catalysis is still in its infancy, MOF catalysts have not yet been adopted by the chemical industry. Much work is needed to expand the library of MOF catalysts, with particular focus on catalytic reactions that are of interest to synthetic chemists, for example, selective reductions, oxidations, $\mathrm{C-H}$ activations, and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C-C}}$ bond cross-coupling reactions. The selected systems in this Account are only some of the exploratory efforts toward this goal. From the chemical industry point of view, more efforts need to be made on developing processes to scale up the synthesis of MOF catalysts and improving their commercial availability, both of which are needed to facilitate their adoption by industry.
The classical approach for catalyst design relies on the laborious synthesis of elaborately designed ligands but faces the challenge of rapid catalyst decomposition through multimolecular pathways. MOFs provide a paradigm-shifting approach to transition metal catalysis. With site isolation, it becomes a reality to design MOF catalysts with unique coordination modes and vacant structures that are inaccessible to homogeneous catalysts. MOF catalysis bridges the gap between chemists’ molecular understanding of transition metal reactivity and its practical application in organic synthesis. With precise knowledge of catalytically active sites in MOFs, we envision the development of practically useful MOF catalysts comprising tailor-made building blocks in the near future.
